9.1. Understanding microplastic pollution
Despite occasional reports of small plastics in the environment from as early as the 1960s [1], it is only in the past 10 years that the scale and pervasiveness of microplastic (MP) pollution has become apparent. Increasing awareness has resulted in almost exponential growth in the number of studies assessing the spread and impacts of MP in the marine environment, and the focus on freshwater and terrestrial habitats is similarly increasing. However, despite the rapid expansion of the field, numerous questions still remain regarding the classification, monitoring, and effect of MPs in the environment. The state of our knowledge might be described as broad but lacking in depth.
9.2. Classifying microplastics
Over the past decade, there have been a number of attempts to formally define the broad category of pollutants identified as “microplastic,” a term previously employed without consistency within or between research fields. More confusingly still, many researchers also employ the term “mesoplastics” to encompass particles that fall at the top end of the MP size range (Fig. 9.1). The classification and categorization of MPs has been variable depending on discipline, resulting in the emergence of common terminology and definitions; however, remaining ambiguity has created some misconceptions by individuals even one step removed from the research (i.e., regulators, media) and may subsequently affect the development of public understanding, meaningful policy, and effective mitigation methods. A framework for plastic definitions was recently suggested by a group of international scientists [2], considering biological impacts and endpoints and other important cross-discipline topics, suggesting that size is not the most important descriptive factor.
