Addressing the problem of standard definitions is critical in enabling comparisons between datasets; examples of inconsistently represented items include cellophane, rubbers, paint particles, and fibers. Polymer chemists believe that rubber, some fibers, and paint particles are not plastic, whereas cellophane, other regenerated cellulose, and semisymmetric materials are included by researchers if the presence of natural fibers can be discounted [3]. This suggests that size should be considered later in the definition of particles. First, we must categorize the physiochemical properties, and size, shape, color, and origin become characterization criteria [2].
This ongoing debate aside, size is currently the most commonly used criterion to define plastics, and size classes are normally recorded as nano-, micro-, macroplastics. Particle size is of ecological relevance because size determines the interaction with biota and environmental fate. The size range of particles referred to as “microplastics” has no clear scientific justification and is often contended. The most frequently used upper limit is 5 mm, as proposed by NOAA [4], partially informed by the availability of small particles for ingestion by marine biota. Initially, no clear lower boundary was proposed, with researchers indicating only the lower limit of the sampling method or processing apparatus, such as sampling net meshes and sieves. For example, manta nets have a number of sampling mesh sizes, commonly 300 μm, and more recently 200, 150, and 50 μm [[5], [6], [7]]. A further issue is when classifying MPs in the number of dimensions measured. The 5 mm boundary refers to the longest axis, and normally a maximum of two dimensions are measured: length and width. However, the dimensions of particles are important and may greatly influence particle fate following ingestion.
The lack of a defined smallest size, measurements which only focus on the largest dimension, and variation between the sampling methods applied results in large variation in the number of MPs recorded, sometimes spanning two or even three orders of magnitude. A 5 mm particle is very different to a 500 μm particle, which is different still to a 50 μm particle. For instance, 5 mm and 500 μm particles may easily pass through digestive tracts and be egested without causing harm, whereas 50 μm are small enough to cross cell boundaries.
One potential alternative is to use conventional units of measurement, such as the SI scale. Therefore, this would mean nanoplastics (1–1000 nm), MPs (1–1000 μm), milliplastics (1–10 mm), centiplastics (1–10 cm), and deciplastics (1–10 dm). Unfortunately, this terminology conflicts with some of the adopted terminology. For example, in the nanomaterials field, nanoplastics are typically considered as 100 nm. As a result, compromise and clear subcategorization are needed [2].
9.3. Origin of plastics
In addition to size, MPs may be classified by their source, a measure clearly important when examining regulatory approaches to limiting plastic pollution. However, determining the origin of many MPs can be unmanageable, and this should only be applied when the origin of the plastic can be reliably determined [2].
Broadly, primary MPs refer to those particles that have been intentionally produced within the MP size range, whereas secondary MPs are formed through fragmentation in the environment or breakdown during use. Those particles intentionally manufactured to be small include preproduction pellets, the feedstock for a large proportion of the plastics industry. Around 350 million tonnes of raw plastics are produced each year [8], and these are transported to manufacturers who melt them down to be converted into individual products or components. Pellets may be lost to the environment during the production, packing, transport, and during use.
Other sources of primary MPs are plastic granules used for a range of functions including cosmetic exfoliation, cleaning products, and sandblasting media. In some applications, particularly domestic usage, the MP particles are discarded immediately following use. In the case of cosmetic microbeads, these products are generally released into wastewater systems. Studies of wastewater treatment works in Southern California have shown a significant contribution of microbeads believed to originate from toothpaste [9]. Recognition of the potential damage caused by cosmetic microbeads has lead to international efforts to minimize their use, including bans in numerous countries.
Secondary MPs originate from a range of diverse sources, both terrestrial and aquatic. There are two distinct phases of secondary production in MP, in-use fragmentation and postuse fragmentation. In-use fragmentation may be typified by tire wear, the formation of microfibres during clothes washing, and the wear of fishing gear [10,11]. Additionally, in-use plastics may be degraded through the action of organisms, such as the production of MPs by boring isopods and polychetes as they produce their burrows in polystyrene (PS) floats [12,13].
Postuse fragmentation may be categorized by the breakdown of lost and discarded plastics. Macroplastic debris may enter the environment via a range of processes, including mismanagement of domestic and industrial wastes, wastewater systems, aeolian transport (being blown by the wind), and direct littering. Exposure to wind, wave action, and UV rays cause large plastic items to become brittle, reducing the durability of plastics and leading to fragmentation into increasingly small pieces. Plastics and MPs may also be further degraded following ingestion. For example, MPs ingested by Krill, Euphausia superba, were seen to reduce in size from >30 to 1 μm [14]. This degradation will continue in the environment, only slowing when exposure to the degradative elements, such as light reduction in the deep sea, and burial in sediment.
9.3.1. How much microplastic is produced?
MP pollution is predominantly a consequence of the proliferation of plastics within industrial and consumer goods, as well as the subsequent mishandling of plastic wastes. The most comprehensive estimates suggest that there could be between 5 and 51 × 1012 (5–51 trillion) particles in the marine environment (Table 9.1) [15]. Examples of these estimations are presented in the table below; the variation observed is predominantly due to inconsistencies between methods.

